{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"This turn resolves the upstream puzzle left open in the previous correction. handle_majority_promises merges exactly one promise log, the one kept as max_promise_sync, and PromiseMetaData orders promises by accept round before accepted index. A follower whose Promise carries a later accept round but a shorter log therefore outranks a follower whose earlier-round Promise carries the accepted StopSign, and the StopSign-bearing log is never merged. That explains how leader 4 could hold a prepare quorum that intersects the StopSign holders yet still send node 3 an AcceptSync without the StopSign: create_log_sync attaches only the leader's own StopSign. It also explains how 38 can still be decided, since handle_accepted counts follower-reported accepted indices through is_chosen, and why no StopSign traffic reaches node 3 afterwards, since resend_messages_leader's StopSign branch is guarded by get_stopsign(). The update registers the promise-ordering source, records the max-merge behavior, adds it as the leading explanation in the question's knowledge, keeps the late-Promise path as a source-consistent alternative, and restates the interpretation with the full chain: AcceptSync to 37 without a StopSign, two sequence-expected Decides clamping at 37, zero PrepareReq, and no repair across 150 fair-delivery rounds. No execution is requested.\"}"}}
